# Mi Pad 2 recovery tools

These helpers use the current Timeshift configuration and expect a Btrfs root with a separately mounted VFAT /boot.

## Backup

Run sudo mp2-backup. It verifies /boot is mounted as VFAT, stores an archive and SHA-256 checksum under /var/lib/mipad2-recovery, then creates a Timeshift Btrfs snapshot using the configured snapshot location.

## Inspect snapshots

Run sudo mp2-recover --status or sudo mp2-recover --list. Listing uses the snapshot device from current Timeshift configuration; no block device is hard-coded.

## Restore

Run sudo mp2-recover. The helper discovers the mounted root device with findmnt, strips any Btrfs subvolume suffix, and uses that device as Timeshift's restore target. Before restoring it refuses unless the root is a single-device Btrfs filesystem; /boot is separately mounted as VFAT; the saved boot archive and SHA-256 file exist and verify; and the archive is a readable zstd tar file.

The verified boot archive is staged on /boot before restoring the root, so an online rollback cannot replace the source archive. Timeshift then restores the root snapshot with --skip-grub, the staged boot archive is extracted, and the temporary copy is removed.

The newest matching Timeshift snapshot is selected and the helper requires typing RECOVER before proceeding. For unattended use, run sudo mp2-recover --yes; this skips only the final confirmation, not the layout and integrity checks.

## Console use and verification

The tools are local-console tools and do not depend on Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, or SSH. Before relying on recovery, run findmnt /, findmnt /boot, sudo mp2-recover --status, and inspect /var/lib/mipad2-recovery/boot-known-good.tar.zst*.

Recovery is fail-closed when the configured layout or backup artifacts do not match.
